Dane Backbier - dbackbie - dbackbie@u.rochester.edu - Project 1 - Infix Calculator

URCalculator works by initially checking if correct arguments where inputted,
then assigning those arguments to variables, then creating new linked lists that
will be used to test my output against the correct output. The program will then try to
create "file" and "ansFile" but catch any FileNotFoundExceptions, scanners are then made for
each file. A while loop will go until "scanner" doesn't have anymore lines, and will create
an expression using the scanner.nextLine() method. First, URCalculator will tokenize the infix
expression so that it can then translate it into postfix. This is done by calling tokenize()
which will return type String[], in this method, a linked list called tokens and StringBuilder
called current are initialized. It will then loop through each character of the input
expression and append the character to current according to whether it's a operand,
operator, function, parenthesis, etc. The point of the flushToken() method is to add
the number or character before adding operators or parenthesis (to keep it in order).
Finally, when the whole expression has been read, tokens is made to an array so that a String[]
can be returned instead of Object[]. After being tokenized URCalculator will translate
the expression into postfix using the translate() method. The translate() method starts
by initializing a queue "output" and a stack "operators", then loop through each item
in the infix String[]. If the item is an operand, it'll enqueue it, if the item is an
operator it'll pop from operators and enqueue to output until operators is empty, it finds
a "(", or finds a lower precedence operator then it pushes item into operators. If item
is a function or "(", it gets pushed to operators. Finally, if item is ")" it'll pop from
operators an enqueue, until it finds a "(". Then it'll pop every operator from operators
and enqueue each into output and return output. For some debugging, URCalculator will
print all of tokens and all of postfix, just to see if any errors are originating from there.
Then URCalculator will evaluate the postfix version of the expression. First, initializing
stack, "stack", and looping through postfix until it's empty. The loop starts by dequeuing from
postfix and assigning the return value to "item". If item is an operand, push onto stack,
if item is a function, check if stack has enough operands, then pop and push back the result (same
with unary). If item is a regular operator, make sure there are two operands left in stack,
then pop both and perform the operation and push the result. When done looping through
postfix, we need to check that there's only 1 item left in stack, and then return that item
as that item should be the answer. Back in main, URCalculator will format the answer so that
it has two decimal points and add it to the "myOutput" linked list, then close the first scanner.
Another while loop, to add each line to correctAnswer linked list then close scanner2. Then more
debugging by printing both myOutput and correctAnswer next to each other. To make sure all my answers
are correct it will check that myOutput and correctAnswer are exactly equal and print accordingly.
Finally, a file will be created called myOutput.txt for each of my answers, which can also be compared
to the postfix_eval_short.txt file for correctness.

List of Files:
infix_expr_short.txt - provided file of expressions
myOutput.txt - text file created by URCalculator.java containing my answers to infix_expr_short.txt
OUTPUT - contains compile and run steps of my code
postfix_eval_short - provided file containing correct answers to infix_expr_short.txt
proj1.pdf - instructions pdf
README
